write("Awakening_Stones", "Awakening Stones", "0", "1", "13",
 "Books 1, 2, 5 (chapter files)",
 "00_Items_Overview.md, Tokens_Stones_and_Relics.md, ../03_Magic_and_Adventuring/Magic_and_Adventuring.md, ../04_Peoples_and_Cultures/Economy_and_Money.md",
 "Single-use stones that awaken an essence ability, found, traded and rewarded by rank and rarity.",
 "- **What they do**: \"An awakening stone that unlocks the power of consumption... Effect: Awakens an essence ability\", requiring an unawakened essence ability (Book 1, Ch11 L17-20). The stone melts into the user's hand \"with little fanfare\" (Book 1, Ch22 L70).\n- **Why they matter**: Rufus tells Jason he cannot \"properly progress towards bronze rank until you awaken every essence ability you have. That's all twenty\" (Book 1, Ch22 L68).\n- **Naming and rarity**: stones are \"unranked\" and carry a rarity, such as the Awakening Stone of the Feast (common, Book 1, Ch11 L17), of Adventure (rare, Book 1, Ch22 L99), of the Stars (epic, Book 1, Ch4 L44) and of the Reaper (legendary, Book 2, Ch57 L31).\n- **Affinity**: the Reaper stone \"sharing affinity with the Reaper\" is used in Jason's awakening ritual with the Reaper token (Book 2, Ch57 L31-33; Book 2, Ch63 L65), and it gives Humphrey, a human, a special attack (Book 2, Ch64 L72).\n- **Divine awakening stones**: a god-made stone is \"transcendent rank, epic\" and bestows a specific aura power (Book 2, Ch13 L60).\n- **Sources**: looting an awakening stone or essence can pay better than the contract to kill the monster (Book 1, Ch57 L21); the Adventure Society gives stones for exceptional service, \"although rarely\", and some open contracts offer them as rewards (Book 1, Ch64 L81); otherwise stones are bought through brokers, almost always at auction, at exorbitant prices (Book 1, Ch64 L82). Brokers in a small city hold the valuable stuff, like essences and stones, for big auctions (Book 1, Ch61 L36).\n- **Family supply**: the Gellers traditionally supply their scions with enough stones to get started, while the rest must be earned (Book 1, Ch64 L80).\n- **Restricted-essence trades**: Adventure Society representatives offer good trades for restricted essences, as when Sophie trades a death essence for two epic stones (Book 2, Ch65 L96).",
 "Awakening stones are the currency of early progression. Scarcity and who can afford a broker's auction drive many scenes in the delta.")

write("Cloud_Constructs_and_Palaces", "Cloud Constructs and Cloud Palaces", "0 for Books 1-2; 1 for Books 5-6", "1", "12",
 "Books 1, 2, 5, 6, 10, 12 (chapter files)",
 "Soul_Bound_Items_and_Regalia.md, ../07_Characters/Emir_Bahadir.md, ../06_Places/Greenstone.md",
 "Emir's cloud ship and palace, and Jason's own cloud houses, yachts and palaces made from his Cloud Flask.",
 "- **Emir's cloud ship**: it arrives at the Greenstone dock and a walkway of cloud connects to the shore as a hole opens in its side (Book 1, Ch101 L64). Moored, it morphs over about ten minutes into a palace of clouds floating on the water (Book 1, Ch101 L103).\n- **Emir's cloud palace**: floating just offshore on the north side of the Island, \"fully exposed to the waves and currents, yet remained as immovable as solid ground\", with wings and towers in blue, purple, orange and gold (Book 1, Ch108 L3). Walls, floors and ceilings glow with absorbed sunlight the palace stores and distributes, and the floors have a bouncy-castle spring (Book 2, Ch1 L3).\n- **Low-magic limits**: out of Greenstone the ship's full functionality is restored when Emir's cloud ship floats above Kurdansk (Book 6, Ch47 L459).\n- **Team base**: Jason's team move into Emir's palace after the contest (Book 2, Ch8 L168).\n- **Jason's Cloud Flask**: it creates buildings and vehicles of cloud, with forms limited by rank, iron cloud houses, bronze cloud vehicles and silver cloud palaces (Book 6, Ch4 L42-50); items inside a returned construct are stored in a dimensional space until another is formed (Book 2, Ch74 L43).\n- **Disguised constructs**: in Venice his family and Dawn stay in a cloud palace disguised as a yacht (Book 6, Ch4 L52).\n- **Asano Village**: Jason's cloud house sits at the bottom of the sea about two kilometres out, reached by a hidden tram (Book 5, Ch5 L106-113).\n- **Uses later**: Hana Shavar runs relief operations from Jason's cloud palace after the messenger attack (Book 10, Ch3 L29); in Cyrion he sits meditating in his cloud palace on the lake (Book 12, Ch48 L26).",
 "A cloud palace is both home and statement; Emir's flair is the model for how much a palace can show off.")

write("The_Builders_Door_and_Fundament_Gate", "The Builder's Door and the Fundament Gate", "2-3", "5", "8",
 "Books 5, 6, 7, 8 (chapter files)",
 "Tokens_Stones_and_Relics.md, ../02_Cosmology_and_Powers/00_Cosmology_Overview.md, ../07_Characters/Mr_North.md",
 "The Builder's astral door, the Fundament Gate that Jason absorbs, and how they let him work in the fundamental realm.",
 "- **The door**: Mr North tells Jason \"It's not a literal door... It's an astral construct with the power to manifest in physical reality in the form of a portal\", and that Jason must absorb it so it cannot be taken from him (Book 5, Ch41 L24-26). Jason notes \"This door is an object of the Builder\" (Book 5, Ch41 L28).\n- **The Fundament Gate**: a ring of rough marble standing stones in a dell, \"transcendent rank, legendary\", whose window lets Jason incorporate it into his Spirit Vault, purging the Builder's influence (Book 5, Ch41 L52-62). It improves his sense of Builder items and adds to Path of Shadows the ability to open a portal to the Fundamental Realm (Book 5, Ch41 L62-64).\n- **Consequences**: Jason used the Builder's door when he flooded himself with magic and later \"accidentally trashed it for parts\" (Book 8, Ch57 L28, L64-67); \"the magic of the Builder's door that Jason had absorbed\" is distinct from the soul-attack trick he learned from the star seed (Book 7, Ch50 L8).\n- **Builder's view**: in Book 6 Shako says that without the Fundament Gate the Builder forged, Jason could not have remade the zone (Book 6, Ch18 L11, L30-36), and Dawn asks for the designs so the World-Phoenix can create a complementary item to anchor the bridge (Book 6, Ch18 L39, L124).\n- **Later items**: the Fundamental Realm Authority Token and the Firmamental Bridge Anchor are looted from the destroyed artefacts (Book 8, Ch50 L94-100); see `Tokens_Stones_and_Relics.md`.",
 "Treat the door as borrowed authority: powerful, traceable to the Builder and always one dispute away from being taken back.")
